  "response": "## Solution\n\n**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n- Prevalence: P(disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(no disease) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|disease) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|no disease) = 0.95, so P(+|no disease) = 0.05\n\n**Compute P(+):**\nP(+) = P(+|disease)·P(disease) + P(+|no disease)·P(no disease)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\n**Apply Bayes' theorem:**\nP(disease|+) = P(+|disease)·P(disease) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.0377941\n\n**Convert to percentage:** 3.7794% ≈ **3.78%**\n\nThis illustrates the classic result that even with a fairly accurate test, screening a low-prevalence population yields many more false positives than true positives, so the positive predictive value stays low.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
